<DocsLayout
  title="Getting started — Kvendra developer docs"
  description="The Kvendra self-hosted quickstart: bring up the reference stack with local embeddings, install the Skills plugin, connect Claude Code over MCP, onboard your project."
>
  <span class="kvd-kicker">Getting Started</span>
  <h1>Quickstart</h1>
  <p>
    One path, in order, that ends in a <strong>first win</strong>: your agent
    planning real work in your project and later recalling the decision by
    meaning. We use the <strong>reference stack</strong> — the ready-made,
    everything-wired-together bundle — with local embeddings, so you get going
    with no signup and nothing leaving your machine. Everything below is
    copy-paste.
  </p>
  <p class="kvd-callout">
    <strong>Shortcut (recommended): let your AI do it.</strong> In Claude Code,
    ask:
    <code>Read https://kvendra.dev/llms.txt and install and configure Kvendra for me.</code>
    It runs every step below and checks each one —
    <a href="/install-with-ai/">see what it does</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The recommended host is <strong>Claude Code</strong>, but anything that
    speaks the Model Context Protocol can drive Kvendra. Prefer to build it
    yourself, step by step — from source, or against your own Postgres? See
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/"><strong>Advanced install</strong></a>. Using
    the hosted service instead? Follow
    <a href="https://app.kvendra.cloud/docs/" rel="noreferrer">app.kvendra.cloud/docs</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <p class="kvd-callout">
    You need <strong>Docker</strong> (Docker Desktop, or
    <a href="https://github.com/abiosoft/colima" rel="noreferrer">Colima</a> on
    macOS; engine ≥ 20.10) with the daemon running, <strong>Docker Compose
    v2</strong>, <strong>git</strong> and <strong>curl</strong>. Check them in
    one go:
  </p>
  <pre><code>docker --version          # Docker version 20.10+ (Desktop or Colima)
docker compose version    # Docker Compose version v2.x  (note: "compose", not "docker-compose")
docker info               # the daemon must be running
git --version
curl --version</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Docker</strong> runs the engine and its database in isolated
      containers, so you do not install Postgres or Node on your host —
      <a href="https://www.docker.com/get-started/" rel="noreferrer"
        >docker.com/get-started</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ollama</strong> runs an embedding model locally so nothing leaves
      your machine — <a href="https://ollama.com" rel="noreferrer">ollama.com</a
      >. The reference stack runs it for you as a container, so there is nothing
      to install separately.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you're spinning up</h2>
  <p>
    A quick look from above. The reference stack brings up these containers,
    wired together on an internal network:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>PostgreSQL 16 + pgvector</strong> — your knowledge base. Entities
      and their embedding vectors live in the same database.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Kvendra Platform engine</strong> — the MCP server your agent
      talks to, on <code>:7777</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A backup sidecar</strong> — a tiny container that takes scheduled
      database dumps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ollama</strong> — the local embeddings runtime, started only when
      you pass <code>--with-ollama</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="kvd-learn-more">
    Want the details of what's in the stack?
    <a
      href="https://github.com/KvendraAI/kvendra-reference-stack"
      rel="noreferrer">see it on GitHub</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Start the stack</h2>
  <p>
    Clone the reference stack somewhere outside your project and bring it up
    with local embeddings:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/KvendraAI/kvendra-reference-stack
cd kvendra-reference-stack
./scripts/up.sh --with-ollama</code></pre>
  <p>
    The script creates <code>.env</code> on the first run, points the engine at
    the local Ollama embedding model (<code>mxbai-embed-large</code>), waits
    until the engine reports healthy and prints the <strong>bearer
    token</strong> you need in step 3. Check it any time:
  </p>
  <pre><code>curl http://localhost:7777/healthz</code></pre>
  <p class="kvd-callout">
    First boot takes 60–90 seconds while pgvector initialises and the engine
    runs its migrations; the embedding model is a one-time, lightweight
    download.
  </p>
  <p class="kvd-callout">
    Prefer hosted embeddings? Put a Kvendra Cloud embeddings key in the
    <code>EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=</code> line of <code>.env</code> and start the
    stack without <code>--with-ollama</code>. You get the key from your Kvendra
    Cloud account at
    <a href="https://app.kvendra.cloud" rel="noreferrer">app.kvendra.cloud</a>.
    Pick one before you store knowledge: vectors from different embedding
    models do not mix, so switching later means re-embedding.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Install the Skills</h2>
  <p>
    Skills turn pipelines into one-command workflows. They install as a Claude
    Code plugin:
  </p>
  <pre><code>/plugin marketplace add KvendraAI/kvendra-skills
/plugin install kvendra-skills@kvendra-marketplace</code></pre>

  <h2 id="connect-claude-code">3. Connect Claude Code</h2>
  <p>
    From <strong>your project's folder</strong>, read the token out of the
    container and register the Platform as an MCP server named
    <code>kvendra-platform</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>TOKEN=$(docker exec kvendra-ref-platform cat /data/auth.token)
claude mcp add --transport http kvendra-platform http://localhost:7777/mcp \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"</code></pre>
  <p>
    The server is registered for that folder. Add <code>--scope user</code> to
    have it in all your projects.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now <strong>restart Claude Code</strong> once — that activates both the
    plugin and the new server. Then verify:
  </p>
  <pre><code>claude mcp list    # kvendra-platform: http://localhost:7777/mcp (HTTP) - ✔ Connected</code></pre>
  <p>
    and ask your agent: <em>“call whoami on kvendra-platform”</em>. A real
    answer means your agent has the 14 knowledge-base tools.
  </p>
  <p class="kvd-callout">
    The plugin also comes with a server for the hosted service,
    <code>kvendra-cloud</code>. You do not need it for self-hosting: leave it
    signed out, or disable it for the project from <code>/mcp</code>. The
    skills then work against <code>kvendra-platform</code>, which serves the
    knowledge-base tools they use under a different server name. A few extras
    of the hosted service (such as export) are not part of the self-hosted
    engine.
  </p>
  <p class="kvd-learn-more">
    Prefer to do every step yourself with full control — your own Postgres,
    building from source, other embedding providers? The whole manual path
    lives in <a href="/docs/self-hosting/">Advanced install</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Onboard your project</h2>
  <p>
    You are connected — now give your agent the structure to work against. The
    natural next step is not generic: it is to <strong>onboard your own
    project</strong> into the knowledge base. Run it in your own repo — the
    reference stack is infrastructure, not your project:
  </p>
  <pre><code>/kvendra-skills:onboard-project &lt;PROJECT_ID&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    It <strong>bootstraps the structure</strong> of your project in the KB — a
    project (PRJ) entity, its components, the shared glossary and standards
    (GLO/STD), and a canonical <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for the repo. It
    auto-detects that it is running against the <strong>local Platform</strong>
    you just brought up. It works for a brand-new project, and for a project
    that <strong>already exists</strong> you can map its pieces one at a time:
  </p>
  <pre><code>/kvendra-skills:onboard-project add-component &lt;name&gt;</code></pre>
  <p class="kvd-callout">
    Precise scope: <code>/kvendra-skills:onboard-project</code> scaffolds the
    project structure and a canonical <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. It does
    <strong>not</strong> crawl or analyse all the code in an existing repo for
    you — you (or your agent, as you work) fill in the detail. The win is that
    the skeleton your agent reads on every session now exists.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Day to day</h2>
  <p>
    Tell Kvendra what you want to do and it routes you to the right workflow:
  </p>
  <pre><code>/kvendra-skills:kvendra &lt;what you want to do&gt;</code></pre>

  <h2>6. Your first win</h2>
  <p>
    With your project onboarded, your agent has the structured context it needs.
    Now see what it is <em>for</em> — this is the payoff, not the healthcheck. In
    Claude Code, ask your agent to plan something small in
    <strong>your own project</strong>:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Plan a small change in this project: add a rate limit to the login endpoint.
    Open a transaction in Kvendra, draft a short roadmap and a couple of tasks
    for it, and link them.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Driven by the Skills, the agent opens a transaction, drafts a roadmap and the
    tasks as typed entities, links them, and commits — so the plan now lives in a
    queryable knowledge base, not in a chat that scrolls away. Here is an
    <strong>illustrative</strong> shape of what you would see:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`# illustrative — your ids and titles will differ
TXN-… opened
  ROAD-…   "Rate-limit the login endpoint"      (draft → active)
  ISSUE-…  "Add token-bucket limiter to /login" (draft → active)
  ISSUE-…  "Add tests for the limit"            (draft → active)
  relations: ISSUE-… part_of ROAD-…
TXN-… committed`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Then, later in the session (or a new one), ask the question that proves the
    point:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    What did we decide about login throttling, and why?
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    The agent finds the roadmap and tasks <em>by meaning</em> and answers from
    them — even though you asked about “throttling” and the entities say “rate
    limit.” That is the whole idea: it stops re-deciding, because the decision is
    recorded and findable. (Illustrative — exactly what surfaces depends on your
    project.)
  </p>

  <h2>7. (Optional, and deferred) Add the CLI security broker</h2>
  <p class="kvd-callout">
    <strong>You do NOT need the CLI to start.</strong> Add it later only when you
    want your agent to run real ops (a git push, a deploy) without exposing your
    tokens. It is a foundational security layer — but optional to get started,
    and everything above works without it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The CLI is currently <strong>pre-alpha</strong>. It is not yet on crates.io
    (a Homebrew formula is pending), so install it from source when you are ready:
  </p>
  <pre><code>cargo install --git https://github.com/KvendraAI/kvendra-cli kvendra</code></pre>
  <p>
    It runs an MCP stdio broker plus a zero-knowledge vault, exposing seven
    primitives — git, github, npm, pypi, http and shell, with an
    <code>unsafe.raw_token</code> escape hatch. Sensitive operations are routed
    through the broker so the model orchestrating the work never sees a raw
    token or key.
  </p>

  <h2>How the pieces fit</h2>
  <p>
    Your agent (Claude Code, the host) talks to the Platform over MCP HTTP on
    port <code>7777</code> — that is the knowledge base. The Skills plugin runs
    on top, calling the same Platform tools, and is your day-to-day. The optional
    CLI adds a second MCP server (stdio) that brokers credentialed operations.
    Start with the Platform and Skills; add the CLI only when you need it.
  </p>
  <p class="kvd-learn-more">
    Want to go step by step instead — from source, or against your own Postgres?
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/">Advanced install</a> has the one-by-one detail.
  </p>
